# Mutants of the event, network and I/O layers' objects, the I/O context's Init and read buffer, the factory and the memory globals (at/atap). Each text
# occurs exactly once in its file. The start-ups' side is the suite atap (gm/atap); both run tests/oracle/oracle_atap.cpp.
#
# Dropped as equivalent, each with its reason:
#  * Clearing a new block's flags in AtIoBufferPool::Init: AtIoBuffer::Init, run on every block straight after, either finds bit 2 clear and leaves the rest,
#    or finds it set and clears the whole block; it then stores the flags word. Either way the block ends the same.
#  * AtNetworkSystem::Init's unused AtApGlobals: its constructor writes only the stack.
#  * AtIoSystemFactory's Init argument: not read.
#  * The I/O layer's second pair of defaults (stack 1, spaces 8): the first pair has just made both non-zero, and AtIoGetFactory neither reads nor writes
#    the config, so the second can never apply.
#  * AtIoSetDeviceHeaps's own setting of AtIoJamFileSystem's heap: AtIoSetFactoryHeaps, which it calls last, sets the same heap from the same field.
#  * Clearing a new block's +0x0C in AtIoBufferPool::Init: AtIoBuffer::Init clears it again on every block straight after.
#  * AtIoGetFactory's Save after the factory's Init: the globals it saves are the ones its Restore wrote from the same struct just before, and the
#    factory's Init does not touch them.
